From 2d7148d42b39f5aa0f35d0d0fb5cb52919c0006e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 12:30:16 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] dos: measure the RUNMANIFEST paced period on SDL's clock, not
 gettimeofday()

Patch 0035's fps_p50/fps_p95 capture read Time::getCurrentTime() --
minorGems' gettimeofday() wrapper -- to time the paced period between
consecutive frames. Every real-hardware run since then reported the
same two values, bit for bit: fps_p50=16.67 (60ms) and fps_p95=9.09
(110ms), on Cirrus, ViRGE and Mach64, on a 486DX2-66, a 486DX2-50 and
an Am5x86-133, while the time(NULL) average moved from 13.69 to 15.02fps
across those same runs (docs/PACER-TIMING-INVESTIGATION.md).

The clock is the cause, not the game. The DJGPP libc reference for
gettimeofday() says: "Although this function returns microseconds for
compatibility reasons, the values are precise to less than 1/20 of a
second only. The underlying DOS function has 1/20 second granularity,
as it is calculated from the 55 ms timer tick count." DOS reports that
tick count in hundredths of a second, and one 54.925ms tick is 5.49
hundredths, so consecutive readings step by 5 or 6. A true ~66.67ms
frame always spans one or two ticks, so its measured period can only
read 50/60ms or 100/110ms. Sort a few thousand of those and the median
is 60ms and the 95th percentile is 110ms on every machine, because
they are properties of the ruler. tests/probes/pacesim.c reproduced
the identical pair in isolation on real hardware (Am5x86-133, 2992
samples, no SDL, rendering or audio: median 60.0000ms, p95 110.0001ms,
max 330.0001ms, i.e. six ticks reported as 33 hundredths). The
near-zero samples patch 0034's reject filter was written against were
the same clock returning the same tick twice.

Fix: read the paced period from SDL_GetTicksNS() -- DJGPP uclock(),
~0.84us resolution, and the clock the absolute-deadline pacer (patch
0030) already converges against, so pacing and measurement now share
one clock family (docs/timing.md's doskutsu precedent). The delta is
signed and bounded on both sides: uclock() is not monotonic on real DOS
hardware (~1 corrupt sample per 1800 frames, SDL/0136's YIELDDBG data),
and a backwards step would otherwise enter the buffer as a 2^64-scale
"period". The existing 1ms lower bound stays; a 5s upper bound is
added; both reject buckets are counted and reported at exit, never
silently dropped. The first loop iteration captures nothing (no
previous post-pacer reading exists yet).

Unchanged: the pacer itself, its frameTime/extraTime arithmetic (which
keeps its own Time::getCurrentTime() read, as before 0035 shared that
read with the capture), the percentile computation and RUNMANIFEST
emission, and the time(NULL) average -- which stays as the independent
witness a self-reported metric needs (docs/timing.md).

Why this matters beyond bookkeeping: docs/BENCHMARK-PLAN.md's KPI is
percentile-based, and the 486DX2-50 campaign it is about to judge
(13.69fps, first datum) cannot be judged on percentiles that cannot
move. This patch is that campaign's Phase 0.

Verified (DOSBox-X, correctness only -- never a performance number): one
full natural life each on three builds of this series. Percentiles now
move with the run instead of reading the ruler: fps_p50=15.00 /
fps_p95=14.83 (high tier), 15.00 / 14.77 (low tier), 15.00 / 14.81
(high tier plus the 0037 diagnostic), against 16.67 / 9.09 on every run
before this patch. 4474 samples captured per 4475-frame life (the first
iteration captures nothing by design), zero rejects in all three
buckets, time(NULL) average 14.97-15.02fps, game time 4:58-4:59.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_017TKEXpxndNddTzMQkSdtBX
